    <!-- Topic 1: Isotopes -->
    <section id="isotopes" class="container reveal">
        <h2 class="neon-text-cyan" style="font-size: 2.5rem; text-align: center;">1. ISOTOPES</h2>
        
        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Definition & Characteristics</h3>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Isotopes</strong> are atoms of the same element that have the <strong>same atomic number</strong> (number of protons) but a <strong>different mass number</strong> (number of neutrons).
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Formation:</strong> Naturally occurring variations in the nucleus where extra neutrons are retained without changing the element's identity.</li>
                <li><strong>Why Atomic Number Remains Same:</strong> The number of protons defines the element. If protons change, the element changes entirely.</li>
                <li><strong>Why Mass Number Changes:</strong> The addition or subtraction of neutrons directly alters the total atomic mass.</li>
                <li><strong>Chemical Properties:</strong> Identical. Chemical behavior is dictated by electrons (specifically valence electrons), which remain the same.</li>
                <li><strong>Physical Properties:</strong> Different. Variations in mass lead to slight differences in density, boiling/melting points, and rate of diffusion.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Important Notes & Memory Tricks</h3>
            <p><strong>Memory Trick:</strong> Iso<strong>TOPES</strong> = Same <strong>P</strong> (Protons).</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> Because they have the same chemical properties, isotopes cannot be separated by standard chemical means. They require physical methods like mass spectrometry or gaseous diffusion.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Applications & Real-life Uses</h3>
            <div class="grid-3">
                <div>
                    <h4 class="neon-text-purple">Medicine</h4>
                    <p>Cobalt-60 is used in radiation therapy for cancer. Iodine-131 is used for thyroid imaging and treatment.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="neon-text-purple">Nuclear Science</h4>
                    <p>Uranium-235 is highly fissionable and used as fuel in nuclear reactors and weapons.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="neon-text-purple">Archaeology</h4>
                    <p>Carbon-14 dating helps determine the age of ancient organic fossils and artifacts.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="neon-text-purple">Agriculture</h4>
                    <p>Phosphorus-32 is used as a tracer to study plant metabolism and fertilizer efficiency.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="neon-text-purple">Industry</h4>
                    <p>Sodium-24 is used to detect leaks in underground oil and water pipes.</p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="neon-text-purple">Scientific Research</h4>
                    <p>Deuterium (H-2) is used in heavy water (D2O) as a moderator in nuclear reactors.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

