    <!-- Topic 2: Isobars -->
    <section id="isobars" class="container reveal">
        <h2 class="neon-text-purple" style="font-size: 2.5rem; text-align: center;">2. ISOBARS</h2>
        
        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Definition & Characteristics</h3>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Isobars</strong> are atoms of <strong>different elements</strong> that have the <strong>same mass number</strong> but <strong>different atomic numbers</strong>.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Formation:</strong> Occurs often as a result of beta decay, where a neutron turns into a proton (or vice versa), keeping the total nucleon count the same but changing the element.</li>
                <li><strong>Chemical Properties:</strong> Entirely different. Since they are different elements with different electron configurations, they react differently.</li>
                <li><strong>Physical Properties:</strong> Mostly different, though their masses are nearly identical.</li>
                <li><strong>Importance & Applications:</strong> Isobars help physicists understand nuclear stability and decay pathways. They are heavily studied in beta decay processes in nuclear physics.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Memory Tricks & Comparisons</h3>
            <p><strong>Memory Trick:</strong> Iso<strong>BARS</strong> = Same <strong>Mass</strong> (Think of a heavy barbell for mass).</p>
            <div class="grid-2 mt-2">
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin:0; border-left-color: var(--neon-purple);">
                    <strong>vs Isotopes:</strong> Isotopes are the SAME element (Same Z). Isobars are DIFFERENT elements (Same A).
                </div>
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin:0; border-left-color: var(--neon-purple);">
                    <strong>vs Isotones:</strong> Isotones share only neutron count. Isobars share total mass (Protons + Neutrons).
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Important Isobars Examples</h3>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Isobar Pair</th>
                            <th>Symbol</th>
                            <th>Atomic No. (Z)</th>
                            <th>Mass No. (A)</th>
                            <th>Protons</th>
                            <th>Neutrons</th>
                            <th>Electrons</th>
                            <th>Explanation</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td rowspan="2">Argon & Calcium</td>
                            <td>⁴⁰Ar</td><td>18</td><td>40</td><td>18</td><td>22</td><td>18</td>
                            <td rowspan="2">Both have a mass of 40, but completely different chemical properties (Noble gas vs Alkaline Earth Metal).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>⁴⁰Ca</td><td>20</td><td>40</td><td>20</td><td>20</td><td>20</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td rowspan="2">Potassium & Argon</td>
                            <td>⁴⁰K</td><td>19</td><td>40</td><td>19</td><td>21</td><td>19</td>
                            <td rowspan="2">Potassium-40 is radioactive and decays into Argon-40; they share mass number 40.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>⁴⁰Ar</td><td>18</td><td>40</td><td>18</td><td>22</td><td>18</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td rowspan="2">Carbon & Nitrogen</td>
                            <td>¹⁴C</td><td>6</td><td>14</td><td>6</td><td>8</td><td>6</td>
                            <td rowspan="2">Carbon-14 undergoes beta decay to form Nitrogen-14. Total nucleons remain 14.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>¹⁴N</td><td>7</td><td>14</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>7</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Topic 3: Isotones -->
    <section id="isotones" class="container reveal">
        <h2 class="neon-text-cyan" style="font-size: 2.5rem; text-align: center;">3. ISOTONES</h2>
        
        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Definition & Characteristics</h3>
            <div class="highlight-box">
                <strong>Isotones</strong> are atoms of <strong>different elements</strong> that contain the <strong>same number of neutrons</strong>, but different atomic numbers and different mass numbers.
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Formation:</strong> They represent different combinations of protons and neutrons in nature that happen to share the exact same neutron count.</li>
                <li><strong>Chemical Properties:</strong> Different (since they are different elements with different electron counts).</li>
                <li><strong>Physical Properties:</strong> Different (different masses and nuclear structures).</li>
                <li><strong>Importance & Applications:</strong> Studying isotones helps scientists understand nuclear shell structure and the magic numbers that contribute to nuclear stability.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Memory Tricks & Comparisons</h3>
            <p><strong>Memory Trick:</strong> Iso<strong>TONES</strong> = Same <strong>N</strong> (Neutrons).</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> To find if two elements are isotones, simply subtract their Atomic Number (Z) from their Mass Number (A). If the result is the same, they are isotones.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="glass-card">
            <h3>Important Isotones Examples</h3>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Isotone Pair</th>
                            <th>Symbol</th>
                            <th>Atomic No. (Z)</th>
                            <th>Mass No. (A)</th>
                            <th>Protons</th>
                            <th>Neutrons</th>
                            <th>Electrons</th>
                            <th>Explanation</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td rowspan="2">Carbon & Nitrogen</td>
                            <td>¹⁴C</td><td>6</td><td>14</td><td>6</td><td>8</td><td>6</td>
                            <td rowspan="2">14 - 6 = 8 Neutrons.<br>15 - 7 = 8 Neutrons.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>¹⁵N</td><td>7</td><td>15</td><td>7</td><td>8</td><td>7</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td rowspan="2">Boron & Carbon</td>
                            <td>¹²B</td><td>5</td><td>12</td><td>5</td><td>7</td><td>5</td>
                            <td rowspan="2">12 - 5 = 7 Neutrons.<br>13 - 6 = 7 Neutrons.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>¹³C</td><td>6</td><td>13</td><td>6</td><td>7</td><td>6</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td rowspan="2">Oxygen & Fluorine</td>
                            <td>¹⁸O</td><td>8</td><td>18</td><td>8</td><td>10</td><td>8</td>
                            <td rowspan="2">18 - 8 = 10 Neutrons.<br>19 - 9 = 10 Neutrons.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>¹⁹F</td><td>9</td><td>19</td><td>9</td><td>10</td><td>9</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

